@@ -69,6 +69,25 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON, so it is valid JSON, and is syntax highlighted by default. The ``indent`` argument sets the number of spaces per indent level (default 4), and ``highlight`` can be set to ``False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]', indent=2)
+
+The JSON is rendered by a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable, which you can also print or log directly. The renderable disables wrapping, so under default print options long lines are cropped to the console width. Pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to keep full lines instead.
+
+The package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function has the same parameters and prints using the global console::
+
+    from rich import print_json
+
+    print_json('{"name": "Rich", "tags": ["console", "json"]}')
+
+Rich also has a command-line entry point which pretty prints a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
